  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 1235 PM Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... An isolated severe thunderstorm is possible within \na larger area of a chance of showers and thunderstorms into the \novernight period, especially in the northeast Piedmont, northern \nCoastal Plain, into the central Coastal Plain.\n\nThe H5 trough continued to remain over the eastern states with the \nmain trough axis offshore of New England and Mid-Atlantic coast. The \nFlow aloft over NC was from the NW and this can be problematic in \nthe summer. Cold air aloft associated with the trough and \nperiods of lift associated with the leading edge of disturbances \nwithin the flow bring unsettled conditions with a chance of \nshowers/storms. \n\nIt has been stable across the region today as the residual low \nstratus associated with the previous days CAD event lingered. Even at \nearly afternoon, only some elevated instability was noted \nincreasing over top of central NC from the NW, with lingering \nstable stratocumulus over much of the region. Temperatures have \nstruggled to even reach the mid 70s to near 80. \n\nAnother shortwave is expected to track from the Great Lakes SE \ninto the Mid-Atlantic region tonight, which is expected \nto bring another chance of showers and thunderstorms across \nportions of VA into north-central and NE NC. The hi-res models are \nstill varying in the placement of the higher POP, but the general \noutlook is for an increase in instability with the approach of the \ndisturbance along with an increase in moisture again tonight. The NW \nflow aloft is also expected to increase and we will be on the \nsouthern end of the stronger winds aloft tonight into Monday. This \nmay support some organized storms overnight which may track into our \nregion from VA. WE will carry 40-60 POP in the northern and eastern \nPiedmont into the Coastal Plain overnight. This activity should exit \nSE early Monday, followed by another chance of PM showers/storms \nlater as additional energy from upstream approaches in the \nincreasing NW flow aloft and MLCapes late in the day as highs reach \nthe mid 80s to lower 90s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \n\nA slight chance of severe thunderstorms (level 2 of 5) for Tuesday \ninto Tuesday night. Damaging wind the main threat.\n\nYet a stronger NW flow and another strong disturbance aloft will \napproach later MOnday night and Tuesday. Models agree that we will \nwe unstable and becoming moreso with heating Tuesday into the upper \n80s to lower 90s. MLCapes may exceed 1500-2000 j/kg. However, early \nmorning thunderstorm complex(s) may send outflow boundaries into the \nregion Tuesday morning as the storms weaken on late night and \nmorning approach from the central Appalachians. Even so, renewed \ndevelopment is expected Tuesday afternoon along a pre-frontal trough \nor residual outflow boundaries over the Piedmont, then move into the \nCoastal Plain and Sandhills. Some of the storms may become severe, \nwith damaging wind the main threat. Although there is currently a \nslight risk for essentially the entire area for severe storms \nTuesday into Tuesday night, this has plenty of potential to change \ngiven much of our forecast Tuesday will rely on what happens with \nthe storms upstream early Tuesday and the placement of outflow and \npre-frontal boundaries to start the day Tuesday. Stay tuned. \n\nIt appears that the best chance of strong to severe storms will be \nduring the afternoon into the evening for our region, as new \ndevelopment should occur by early afternoon on the said boundaries \nand last well into the night as they spread SE.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3... A return to a more dry pattern on Wednesday, with \nrain chances increasing next weekend. \n\nAfter the frontal passage Tuesday night into Wednesday, high \npressure will build in as the front stalls to our south. Each \nafternoon will have high temperatures in the upper 80s to lower 90s, \nwith lows dipping into the mid 60s to lower 70s. These temperatures \nare normal for this time of year. The front will also usher in drier \nair, with PWs around 1.2 in Wednesday through Friday. This will \ndiminish rain chances, however a very isolated shower cannot be \nruled out each afternoon. Rain chances look to increase next weekend \nas the front starts to retrograde back northward and a mid-level \ndisturbance approaches the region from the west.",
